## Best Practices

### Performance

1. ✅ Use LODs for distant objects
2. ✅ Nanite for complex geometry
3. ✅ Virtual textures for large worlds
4. ✅ Cull invisible objects
5. ✅ Profile with Unreal Insights

### Organization

```
Content/
├── Characters/
│   ├── Player/
│   └── Enemies/
├── Environment/
│   ├── Props/
│   └── Materials/
├── UI/
├── Audio/
├── Blueprints/
│   ├── Core/
│   └── Gameplay/
└── Maps/
```

### Coding Standards

```cpp
// Use UPROPERTY for GC and reflection
UPROPERTY()
UStaticMeshComponent* Mesh;

// Always check pointers
if (MyActor != nullptr)
{
    MyActor->DoSomething();
}

// Use soft references for assets
TSoftObjectPtr<UStaticMesh> MeshAsset;
```

## Summary

1. ✅ Master Blueprint basics first
2. ✅ Learn C++ for performance-critical code
3. ✅ Understand material system
4. ✅ Leverage Lumen for lighting
5. ✅ Use proper animation workflows
6. ✅ Follow gameplay framework patterns

## Conclusion

Unreal Engine 5 provides powerful tools for creating stunning games. Start with Blueprints, gradually incorporate C++, and leverage UE5's advanced features like Nanite and Lumen.

Happy developing!
